    "rule": "For p=a*x^2+b*x+c, explained a>=0, c>=0 and 4*a*c-b^2>=0 imply p>=0. Positive determinants or exact positive constant separation can prove strict positivity.",
    "coefficient_proofs": "Exact p=q*h+r identities combine active polynomial signs with quotient/remainder signs. Divisors must have a nonzero rational constant leading coefficient; no possibly-zero symbolic expression is divided out.",
    "degeneracy": "The c>=0 condition remains necessary when a=0. Weak nonnegativity never alone refutes equality or a non-strict boundary.",
    "state": "Per-call bounded exact arithmetic and complete premise reasons; no proof state persists across checks or backtracking.",
    "preserved": "Original model checking, exact arithmetic, public solver semantics and LRA/LIA backtracking policies."
